# Songhealth production application container (Feature 001, T020).
#
# Authority:
# - specs/001-platform-foundation/tasks.md T020 — the single Astro + EmDash
#   application Quadlet with the persistent media volume, private network,
#   proxy-facing loopback publication, external secrets/configuration,
#   restart behavior, and startup ordering that runs T013's
#   exact-build-manifest status/apply/check migration gate before the
#   application is accepted ready; block readiness on migration failure
#   without adding a migration container, service, or orchestration layer
# - docs/website-modernization-docs/current/10-system-blueprint.md §2 (pin
#   dependency/image versions in production deployment definitions; no
#   unbounded `latest`), §3.1 (rootless Podman + systemd Quadlet baseline:
#   app + PostgreSQL on one private network, only the application published
#   on the proxy-facing loopback boundary, secrets never baked into images,
#   Quadlet defines startup/restart and health/readiness), §20/§20.1
#   (environment-specific values stay external; the native runtime input
#   contract PGHOST/PGPORT/PGDATABASE/PGUSER/PGPASSWORD,
#   EMDASH_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL, EMDASH_SITE_URL, HOST/PORT,
#   EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY; media at the stable ./media path)
# - specs/001-platform-foundation/plan.md — "Technical Context"/"Configuration
#   and secrets" (pin the built application image by explicit version AND
#   immutable digest; non-secret values may use an external environment file;
#   secret-bearing values stay in operator-managed Podman/systemd secrets or
#   equivalent host-protected files), "Production topology and durability"
#   (publish only the application port to the proxy-facing host boundary; a
#   missing expected attachment or an accidentally empty replacement store
#   must never appear as a healthy platform), "Runtime verification and
#   readiness" (the native migration sequence is a deployment/startup gate
#   before the application is declared ready; recurring readiness is private
#   and non-mutating; Quadlet health + Notify=healthy make the state
#   observable)
# - accepted contracts consumed as-is, never reconstructed here:
#   deploy/Containerfile (T017 single production OCI runtime, whose /app tree
#   is deliberately writable by its non-root `node` user so this unit can run
#   the gate's exact in-place rebuild), deploy/quadlet/songhealth.network and
#   songhealth-media.volume (T018 private network and persistent media
#   volume), deploy/quadlet/songhealth-db.container (T019 private-only
#   PostgreSQL, resolved on the network as systemd-songhealth-db),
#   tests/operations/verify-startup.sh driven through package.json's
#   committed verify:startup mapping (T013 startup-acceptance gate:
#   S1 input/identity validation -> S2 exact build + native manifest ->
#   S3 status/fingerprint inspection -> S4 fingerprint-guarded apply ->
#   S5 check -> S6 bounded disposable media write/read/remove probe),
#   scripts/verify-readiness.mjs (T012 private non-mutating aggregate
#   readiness helper, also wired into container health below),
#   tests/operations/verify-foundation.sh (T016 harness; it resolves this
#   container as systemd-songhealth-app, so ContainerName is deliberately
#   NOT set and keeps the Quadlet default naming)
#
# Image identity (rework r1 finding 1; digest re-pinned in rework r2 to the
# retained inspectable canonical artifact): the full immutable production
# identity is pinned below as explicit repository name + explicit version +
# immutable digest of the canonical build of THIS checkout's T017
# Containerfile (podman build -f deploy/Containerfile -t
# localhost/songhealth-app:0.0.1 .; the build's only base is itself pinned
# by version and immutable digest inside the Containerfile). 0.0.1 is the
# repository application version (package.json). The digest is the exact
# build this deployment definition accepts — podman resolves the digest
# against local image storage, so a different or rebuilt image never
# satisfies it and container creation fails closed; changing the accepted
# build therefore requires changing this pinned identity. Because default
# image builds are not bit-reproducible across build times, the accepted
# artifact is RETAINED in the deployment verification runtime's rootless
# Podman storage (inspectable as localhost/songhealth-app@sha256:<digest>)
# so the pinned identity can be independently verified; the r1 build was
# discarded during that round's teardown and is superseded by this
# retained artifact. The tag is kept alongside the digest for
# human-readable identity only; the digest is the enforcement. Never
# `latest`, never a bare mutable tag.
#
# External runtime inputs (never committed; every absence fails closed):
#
# - ~/.config/containers/systemd/songhealth-app.env — the operator-managed,
#   host-protected environment file loaded at the [Service] level (the %h
#   specifier is expanded by user systemd; no host-specific absolute path is
#   committed), supplying the approved native runtime inputs:
#     HOST=127.0.0.1        loopback application address for the accepted
#                           T012 readiness contract (see the server-bind
#                           note below)
#     PORT=4321             application port; must equal the published
#                           container port below — enforced before container
#                           creation by an ExecCondition skip on mismatch
#     PGHOST=systemd-songhealth-db
#                           the T019 container's resolvable name on the
#                           private songhealth network
#     PGPORT=5432           PostgreSQL port inside the private network
#     PGDATABASE=<target application database>
#     PGUSER=<least-privilege application role — enforced below>
#     PGPASSWORD=<application role credential>
#     EMDASH_SITE_URL=<absolute production origin>
#   Only these eight named variables are forwarded into the container
#   (Environment= bare-name forwarding below); no other key in the file
#   reaches it. PGPASSWORD is secret-bearing and lives in this host-protected
#   file rather than a Podman file-secret because the accepted recurring
#   readiness contract reads it from the container environment itself:
#   scripts/verify-readiness.mjs is executed by the T016 harness through
#   `podman exec` and by the container healthcheck below, both of which
#   inherit only the container's configured environment — a file-mounted
#   secret can never satisfy those contracts (plan.md permits exactly this:
#   operator-managed Podman/systemd secrets or equivalent host-protected
#   files). The file must be operator-protected (0600); systemd does not
#   expose EnvironmentFile values in unit definitions, `systemctl show`, or
#   the journal.
#
# - the Podman secrets `songhealth-migration-database-url` and
#   `songhealth-encryption-key` — the two most sensitive inputs (the
#   migration connection URL with its embedded password, and the EmDash
#   encryption key), injected through the native Quadlet Secret= mechanism
#   and mounted read-only at /run/secrets/<name>. They are read by the
#   container's own startup command below and exported only into the
#   application process environment: they never appear in this file, in the
#   container's configured environment (so `podman inspect` cannot leak
#   them), in Git, or in any image layer, and they are not exec-visible.
#   Container creation fails when either secret does not exist.
#
# Operator-managed persistence prerequisite (rework r1, finding 3): the
# persistent media store must already exist as an explicitly provisioned
# store. The T018 volume unit (and Podman generally) creates a missing named
# volume as an empty replacement (`podman volume create --ignore`), which a
# mount-only check cannot distinguish from the operator's existing store —
# so this unit additionally requires the operator-created store marker
# `.songhealth-media-store` at the root of the media volume. The operator
# creates the marker ONCE when provisioning the store (a small non-secret
# provisioning record, e.g. an ISO timestamp and host identifier, owned by
# the runtime user). The marker travels with the persistent bytes only: a
# deleted-and-recreated volume, a wiped store, or an unprovisioned
# deployment has no marker, and this unit refuses to start — it never
# creates, initializes, or repairs the store itself. The same
# deleted-volume recreation hazard exists for the T019 database volume;
# that store's enforcement belongs to the T019 unit and is noted for
# review, not changed here.
#
# Least-privilege application-role prerequisite (rework r1 finding 4,
# completed in rework r2): the approved contract (T019/T020) is ONE stable,
# non-superuser, least-privilege application role with database CONNECT,
# schema USAGE/CREATE, DML, and ownership/inherited ownership of the EmDash
# objects — provisioned by the operator against the running T019 database,
# never the bootstrap administrator (POSTGRES_USER, initialized WITH
# SUPERUSER by the official image) and never the bootstrap secret
# songhealth-db-password. T013 proves identity agreement and migration
# state, not role privileges, and a merely-applied migration state or a
# SELECT-only probe establishes no CMS write permission, so before any
# migration mutation runs, this unit's startup command enforces the
# privilege prerequisite itself through the application's own pinned pg
# client, connecting exactly as the configured PG* application identity:
# the connecting role must exist, must NOT be SUPERUSER or hold CREATEDB or
# CREATEROLE (which also structurally rejects the bootstrap administrator),
# and must hold CONNECT on the target database plus USAGE and CREATE on
# schema public — and, completing the contract, every EXISTING EmDash
# object is verified read-only for BOTH ownership/inherited ownership AND
# effective required DML: EmDash 1.1.0 creates its tables and sequences
# unqualified, so they live in schema public of the dedicated application
# database, and each existing ordinary/partitioned table or sequence there
# must be owned by the connecting role directly or through role membership
# (pg_has_role USAGE — the native ownership/inherited-ownership test) AND
# hold its full required DML set (tables: SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE;
# sequences: SELECT, USAGE, UPDATE — distinct privileges per PostgreSQL 18
# ddl-priv). Neither condition alone satisfies the approved contract:
# PostgreSQL 18 GRANT keeps ownership and grantable DML distinct — an owner
# can revoke its own ordinary DML privileges (owned with no effective DML),
# and a non-owner can hold full grants without ownership — so both invalid
# shapes are rejected, each naming the violated condition(s). A database
# with no such objects yet is the legitimate pre-first-migration state and
# passes; after the first native apply the role owns the objects it created
# with their privileges intact. Any failure names the failed prerequisite
# or object only
# (identity components are the documented non-secret ones; credentials and
# connection errors never print values) and blocks the start before the
# T013 gate's apply step. The migration URL must still identify the same
# intended database as the PG* inputs; the gate's S1 enforces that
# agreement by non-secret identity components only, and S3 re-proves it
# against the inspected native target label. This unit provisions nothing,
# grants nothing, repairs nothing, and initializes nothing.
#
# Startup sequence (one container; no migration container, migration
# service, custom server, or orchestration layer exists): the image's native
# entrypoint receives one `sh -c` payload that, in order,
#   1. proves the expected persistent media attachment is live: /app/media
#      must be a real mount point (the same /proc/mounts field-2 primitive
#      the T016 harness uses). The T017 image ships an empty placeholder
#      directory at /app/media, so an omitted Volume= attachment would
#      otherwise silently present a writable ephemeral replacement store as
#      healthy — plan.md forbids exactly that. Refusal names the missing
#      attachment and exits before anything else runs.
#   2. proves the store is the operator-provisioned existing store: the
#      store marker above must exist and be readable. A missing marker (an
#      unprovisioned or replaced store) refuses to start with the
#      prerequisite named — no initialization, no repair.
#   3. reads the two Podman secrets into the process environment (missing
#      secret files surface as named missing inputs in the gate's S1);
#   4. enforces the least-privilege application-role prerequisite above —
#      role attributes, database CONNECT, schema USAGE/CREATE, and
#      read-only verification that every existing EmDash object in schema
#      public carries BOTH required ownership (direct or through role
#      membership) AND effective required DML — before the gate runs and
#      therefore strictly before any migration mutation, printing one
#      secret-safe verification line (with the verified existing-object
#      count) on success;
#   5. runs the accepted T013 startup-acceptance gate as-is through the
#      committed verify:startup mapping — input/identity validation, the
#      exact application rebuild and its native manifest checks, native
#      status -> inspected-fingerprint-guarded apply -> post-apply
#      inspection -> check, and the bounded disposable media
#      write/read/remove probe (the only migration/write probes; they stay
#      startup-only). Any failure exits nonzero, the container never starts
#      the application, and the unit follows its restart policy; the gate's
#      full [S1..S6] PASS markers land in this unit's journal for every
#      start.
#   6. only then execs the native standalone server emitted by that exact
#      build (dist/server/entry.mjs), with the in-container bind widened to
#      0.0.0.0 for this one process.
#
# Server bind note (verified against rootless Podman 5.8.4 on the
# verification host, both for the bridge network used here and for pasta):
# a container process bound to the container's own loopback is NOT reachable
# through a published host port — rootless port forwarding delivers to the
# container's network address (pasta(1) likewise targets the namespace's
# public address unless --host-lo-to-ns-lo is set, and that option does not
# apply to bridge-network containers). The HOST input above stays the
# loopback application address the accepted T012 helper probes
# (http://127.0.0.1:4321/ inside the container), and a 0.0.0.0 bind answers
# that loopback probe — so this unit starts the server process with
# HOST=0.0.0.0 as explicit deployment configuration while the external
# boundary remains loopback-only: PublishPort below binds the host side to
# 127.0.0.1 only, and the container's second interface is the private
# songhealth network shared only with PostgreSQL. No unintended interface
# is exposed (.env.example: production HOST must match the container/proxy
# boundary and must not expose an unintended interface).
#
# T016 verification status (canonical rebaseline): the two limitations this
# unit previously recorded as outstanding are RESOLVED at source level by
# the accepted canonical T016 harness and specification, and the outdated
# statements are withdrawn: (a) C1 — the accepted harness now recognizes
# the approved in-container startup-gate wiring form, including a
# startup-payload identity contract that requires this unit's Exec payload
# to be BYTE-EQUAL to the accepted T020 payload authority embedded in the
# harness; this file preserves that payload byte-for-byte (verified at
# rebaseline), so the wiring contract is satisfied without changing either
# side; (b) C7 — the accepted Feature 001 specification (Session
# 2026-10-09 clarification) recognizes the Product Owner-approved STARTUP
# REFUSAL evidence class: when this unit refuses to start because the
# persistent media attachment is absent, the harness accepts the
# invocation-scoped, media-specific refusal emitted by step 1 above
# (guard diagnostic shape: volume ... not attached at /app/media ...
# refusing to start) as the operational not-ready evidence, alongside
# genuine T012 results from a running container. Runtime verification of
# both stages — like all deployed behavior — remains pending and belongs
# to T022's separate authorization; nothing here claims runtime
# acceptance.

[Unit]
Description=Songhealth Astro + EmDash application (T013 startup gate, least-privilege role prerequisite, private network, loopback-only publication)
# Hard ordering and dependency on the T019 PostgreSQL unit: starting this
# unit starts the database first, and the database unit's own Notify=healthy
# semantics mean "started" there is "healthy". The network and media-volume
# dependencies are generated natively by the Network=/Volume= unit
# references below.
#
# T019 skip semantics (canonical rebaseline): Requires=/After= is ordering
# convenience only, NOT proof that the intended database is operationally
# usable — a skipped PostgreSQL ExecCondition can complete its systemd start
# job without propagating an error through this dependency edge, and T019
# health or completed store provisioning does not by itself prove the
# application identity is provisioned. The authoritative verification is
# this unit's own in-container prerequisites (step 4: least-privilege
# application-role connectivity and privilege gate) and the T013 gate's
# S1/S3 identity checks, which fail closed regardless of the dependency
# edge. No T019 behavior is modified or reimplemented here.
Requires=songhealth-db.service
After=songhealth-db.service

[Container]
# The single T017 production application image, pinned by the FULL immutable
# identity: explicit repository + explicit version + the immutable digest of
# the canonical build of this checkout's deploy/Containerfile (see the
# image-identity note above). Only that exact image satisfies this
# reference; any other or rebuilt image fails container creation closed.
Image=localhost/songhealth-app:0.0.1@sha256:4cfccf41d4e10fc32f1f9dc76d5b7a69bed049b7b03556eb82059f5278de7407

# Attach exclusively to the accepted T018 private application network
# (NetworkName=songhealth). The Quadlet reference generates
# Requires=/After= on songhealth-network.service; PostgreSQL is reachable
# only here and publishes no host port of its own (T019).
Network=songhealth.network

# Persistent public-publishable media: the accepted T018 volume
# (VolumeName=songhealth-media) mounted at the image's canonical stable
# application path /app/media (blueprint §20.1; no MEDIA_DIRECTORY input
# exists). The Quadlet reference generates a dependency on
# songhealth-media-volume.service; container recreation replaces only the
# ephemeral container layer, never the named volume. The operator-managed
# existing-store marker is enforced by the startup command below.
Volume=songhealth-media.volume:/app/media

# The only publication in this deployment: the application port on the host
# loopback, for the external HTTPS/reverse-proxy boundary. The host side is
# bound to 127.0.0.1 only — never all interfaces — and the committed
# container port (4321) is the single authority for the whole port
# configuration: the standalone server, the T012 readiness probe, and the
# healthcheck all consume the same inherited PORT input, and an
# ExecCondition below rejects any PORT that disagrees with this publication
# before any container exists, so listener, probe, and publication can
# never silently diverge. PostgreSQL is not published in any form.
PublishPort=127.0.0.1:4321:4321

# Approved native runtime inputs forwarded by bare name — each value is
# inherited from the unit environment that user systemd loaded from the
# external environment file, exactly as the accepted T019 unit forwards its
# identity inputs. No other key from that file reaches the container.
Environment=HOST
Environment=PORT
Environment=PGHOST
Environment=PGPORT
Environment=PGDATABASE
Environment=PGUSER
Environment=PGPASSWORD
Environment=EMDASH_SITE_URL

# Operator-managed Podman secrets for the two most sensitive inputs, mounted
# read-only at /run/secrets/<name> and read by the startup command below.
# Missing secrets fail container creation.
Secret=songhealth-migration-database-url
Secret=songhealth-encryption-key

# One startup payload (see the startup-sequence note above): media
# attachment guard -> existing-store marker guard -> secret reads ->
# least-privilege application-role prerequisite -> the accepted T013 gate
# via the committed verify:startup mapping -> exec the native standalone
# server with the deployment's in-container 0.0.0.0 bind. Every failure
# exits nonzero before the application starts; each prerequisite's verdict
# and the gate's per-stage output land in this unit's journal.
Exec=sh -c "grep -q ' /app/media ' /proc/mounts || { echo songhealth-app: required persistent media volume songhealth-media is not attached at /app/media - refusing to start >&2; exit 1; }; test -r /app/media/.songhealth-media-store || { echo songhealth-app: operator-managed existing media store marker .songhealth-media-store is missing at /app/media - the persistent store was not provisioned or has been replaced - refusing to start >&2; exit 1; }; IFS= read -r EMDASH_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL </run/secrets/songhealth-migration-database-url; IFS= read -r EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY </run/secrets/songhealth-encryption-key; export EMDASH_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL EMDASH_ENCRYPTION_KEY; node -e 'const pg=require(`pg`);const c=new pg.Client({host:process.env.PGHOST,port:Number(process.env.PGPORT),database:process.env.PGDATABASE,user:process.env.PGUSER,password:process.env.PGPASSWORD,connectionTimeoutMillis:5000,query_timeout:5000});const fail=m=>{console.error(`songhealth-app: `+m);process.exit(1)};(async()=>{await c.connect();const r=await c.query(`select rolsuper s,rolcreatedb cd,rolcreaterole cr,has_database_privilege(current_user,current_database(),$q$CONNECT$q$) dbc,has_schema_privilege(current_user,$q$public$q$,$q$USAGE$q$) su,has_schema_privilege(current_user,$q$public$q$,$q$CREATE$q$) sc from pg_roles where rolname=current_user`);const b=r.rows[0];if(!b)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role has no pg_roles row`);if(b.s)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role is SUPERUSER - the bootstrap administrator is not the application identity`);if(b.cd)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role has CREATEDB`);if(b.cr)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role has CREATEROLE`);if(!b.dbc)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role lacks CONNECT on the target database`);if(!b.su)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role lacks USAGE on schema public`);if(!b.sc)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: the connecting role lacks CREATE on schema public`);const o=await c.query(`select case c.relkind when $q$S$q$ then $q$sequence$q$ else $q$table$q$ end kind,c.relname obj,pg_has_role(current_user,c.relowner,$q$USAGE$q$) owned,case c.relkind when $q$S$q$ then has_sequence_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$SELECT$q$) and has_sequence_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$USAGE$q$) and has_sequence_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$UPDATE$q$) else has_table_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$SELECT$q$) and has_table_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$INSERT$q$) and has_table_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$UPDATE$q$) and has_table_privilege(current_user,c.oid,$q$DELETE$q$) end dml from pg_class c join pg_namespace n on n.oid=c.relnamespace where c.relkind in ($q$r$q$,$q$p$q$,$q$S$q$) and n.nspname=$q$public$q$`);await c.end();for(const x of o.rows){const p=[];if(!x.owned)p.push(`not owned by the connecting role directly or through role membership`);if(!x.dml)p.push(`lacking effective required DML privileges`);if(p.length>0)fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: existing EmDash `+x.kind+` `+x.obj+` in schema public is `+p.join(` and `))}console.error(`songhealth-app: least-privilege application-role prerequisite verified: non-superuser, no CREATEDB, no CREATEROLE, database CONNECT, schema public USAGE and CREATE, and `+o.rows.length+` existing EmDash relation(s) in schema public pass required ownership (direct or inherited) and full DML verification`)})().catch(e=>fail(`least-privilege application-role prerequisite failed: `+((e&&e.code)||`error`)))' && npm run verify:startup && HOST=0.0.0.0 exec node dist/server/entry.mjs"

# Native container health = the accepted T012 aggregate readiness, wired
# verbatim (rework r1, finding 2): the helper runs inside the container
# (private boundary, no public endpoint), consumes only the container's own
# configured environment, and performs its bounded non-mutating checks —
# loopback SSR probe with the expected marker, bounded PostgreSQL
# connectivity, and media-mount presence/readability — self-terminating
# within its own 8s overall bound and exiting nonzero on any not-ready
# result. A mere HTTP 200 is therefore never sufficient: with the database
# unreachable or the media mount unreadable, health fails while the SSR
# page may still be served. The migration/write probes are NOT part of
# health — they remain startup-only in the T013 gate above, so recurring
# health never mutates persistent state. HealthTimeout exceeds the
# helper's own wall-clock bound; failures during the startup gate fall
# inside HealthStartPeriod and do not count. Notify=healthy postpones the
# generated unit's startup notification until this aggregate check passes,
# so user systemd never considers this service started before the gate has
# passed AND the application is actually, completely ready.
HealthCmd=node scripts/verify-readiness.mjs
HealthInterval=10s
HealthTimeout=15s
HealthRetries=3
HealthStartPeriod=300s
Notify=healthy

# Rootless user-systemd lifecycle behavior — no alternative orchestration
# layer exists, and no boot-time enablement is defined here: the unit runs
# when the operator starts or enables it through user systemd.
#
# The environment file supplies the runtime inputs at the systemd level. A
# missing file fails the unit load before any container exists, and the
# ExecCondition gates skip the unit — exit 1-254 semantics: skipped, not
# failed, and not restarted — when a required input is missing, empty, or
# (for PORT) in disagreement with the committed publication port, naming
# the field or conflict only (values are never printed). In every gated
# case no container is created, no secret is read, and the healthy
# notification cannot fire.
#
# Port consistency (rework r4, F01): the PORT input must equal the
# committed publication container port 4321 above. The equality
# ExecCondition rejects any conflicting PORT before startup side effects;
# PORT is a documented non-secret identity component, so its expansion in
# this one argument is safe (no secret is involved).
#
# Secret-safe presence check (rework r4, F03): PGPASSWORD must be
# non-empty in the effective environment systemd loaded from the
# EnvironmentFile, but its value must never be interpolated into a host
# command argument. The ':' command prefix disables systemd's command-line
# environment variable substitution entirely (systemd.service(5) v259: an
# Exec prefixed with ':' passes a literal "$USER"), so the shell below
# receives the literal text test -n "$PGPASSWORD" and its BUILT-IN test
# inspects the variable inside the process environment the shell inherits
# from the unit: the credential appears in no argv, no log line, and no
# diagnostic. ConditionEnvironment= was considered and rejected: it checks
# the service manager's environment, not EnvironmentFile= values
# (systemd.unit(5)); a pattern match on the file was rejected by the
# Product Owner: quoted and multi-line EnvironmentFile values are not
# reliably validated that way (systemd.exec(5)). The value necessarily
# remains in the service's own process environment — inherent to any
# environment-injection mechanism, including the container runtime itself —
# but never in world-readable command-line metadata.
#
# Precise input validation (port ranges, URL form, PG*/URL identity
# agreement) belongs to the accepted T008 configuration boundary and the
# T013 gate's S1, which fail closed inside the container.
[Service]
EnvironmentFile=%h/.config/containers/systemd/songhealth-app.env
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${HOST}"
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test "${PORT}" = 4321
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${PGHOST}"
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${PGPORT}"
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${PGDATABASE}"
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${PGUSER}"
ExecCondition=:/usr/bin/sh -c 'test -n "$PGPASSWORD"'
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/test -n "${EMDASH_SITE_URL}"

# The unit returns after any unplanned container exit (application crash,
# failed startup gate, OOM kill, external signal shutdown); an explicit
# `systemctl --user stop` is never restarted, and systemd's built-in start
# rate limiting bounds any retry loop. A failed gate therefore never
# escalates to a healthy state — it can only retry or stay down.
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s

# Startup timeout budget (rework r4 F02; comment corrected in rework r5 per
# the Product Owner decision). The verified part is the accepted T013
# sequence as committed — never shortened or bypassed here: the exact
# application build is bounded at 900s plus a 30s kill-after (930s), and
# the four sequential bounded native CLI calls — S3 status, S4 apply, S4
# post-apply status, S5 check — at 600s plus a 30s kill-after each (2520s),
# for 3450s of bounded command allowance. TimeoutStartSec=3600s is the
# Product Owner accepted operational policy ceiling for each start
# attempt; the remaining 150s is an overhead reserve for everything else —
# the role-prerequisite connection and its two bounded queries (10s bounds
# each), container start and npm/npx startup overhead, the T013 S6 bounded
# media claim/write/read/remove operations, standalone server boot, and
# the first healthcheck pass that releases the Notify=healthy startup
# notification — and that reserve is UNVERIFIED: the S6 filesystem
# operations and the standalone server startup have no independently
# proven wall-clock limits, so no overall worst-case duration is claimed
# here. A start attempt whose legitimate progress exceeds the ceiling will
# be terminated by systemd; that possibility is accepted by the Product
# Owner decision rather than eliminated by this unit. Actual startup
# durations, and whether this ceiling accommodates them, remain subject to
# separately authorized runtime validation.
TimeoutStartSec=3600s
